// Abandoning a parked frame whose owner is gone must not try to run its
// defer() handlers.
//
// A parked frame can hold pending defer()s, and there are two routes that
// abandon one. The eager route, taken when the object is destructed while the
// frame is still parked, discards them and says why: they are funptrs owned
// by the object being destructed, and call_function_pointer() refuses those.
//
// The other route is reached when the resume was already QUEUED before the
// destruct: the drain delivers it, resume_coroutine() finds the owner gone,
// and abandons. That route used to ask for the defers to run anyway -- so
// every one of them produced a driver error ("Owner (...) of function pointer
// is destructed") through the mudlib error handler, one per defer per
// abandoned frame, naming a cause no mudlib author can act on. A mass
// destruct produced a burst of them.
//
// Same situation, so it now gets the same answer as its sibling: the decision
// is made from whether LPC can still run in that object, not from which route
// asked (AGENTS.md section 15). The same rule covers a recompiled or
// replaced-program owner, where the funptrs are stale rather than destructed.
//
// Be straight about what this file can and cannot prove. The refusal is
// SILENT from LPC: pop_control_stack() runs defers through
// safe_call_function_pointer(), which swallows the error, so on the unfixed
// driver there is no failure to record and nothing to assert against. What
// the file does is pin the SETUP -- that the frame really is parked, its
// resume really is queued, and its owner really is destructed before the
// drain gets to it -- which is the part that silently stops being true when
// the surrounding machinery changes. Instrumenting free_coroutine() confirms
// this scenario reaches the branch: run_lpc=1 with a destructed owner, which
// the fix turns into can_run=0. The fix itself rests on matching its sibling
// route (AGENTS.md section 13's code-symmetry argument, which it explicitly
// allows where no observable regression route exists).

nosave object worker;
nosave promise gate;

private void done() {
  // Assert the SETUP, not the fix: the fix is the absence of an error, and a
  // file whose passing run records no check at all cannot be told apart from
  // one that never reached the scenario. These two say the frame really was
  // parked, queued and abandoned.
  ASSERT2(!worker, "the worker should have been destructed while its resume was queued");
  ASSERT_EQ(1, promise_status(gate));
  RELEASE_LATCH("abandon defer");
}

void do_tests() {
  gate = promise_create();

  EXPECT_LATCH("abandon defer");

  worker = clone_object("/clone/async_defer_park");
  worker->set_gate(gate);
  worker->park();  // runs to the await and parks, with a defer outstanding

  // Queue the resume FIRST, then destruct: that is what routes this frame
  // through resume_coroutine()'s abandon rather than the eager sweep.
  promise_resolve(gate, 0);
  destruct(worker);
  worker = 0;

  // Late enough that the drain has delivered the queued resume and taken the
  // abandon path.
  call_out((: done :), 2);
}
